# Valve's arm64 Steam client: the CHANNEL it is downloaded from (installed from Main menu > Emulators
# > Steam Client by steam-install).
#
# Read by /usr/lib/retroforge/steam-bootstrap. The client build is not pinned to a version here: the
# channel is a rolling pointer Valve moves, and the bootstrap installs whatever Valve publishes on it
# when it downloads, checked against the sha2 the channel's manifest gives. The client is not in the
# image (a root slot is 2 GB), so it goes into the player's home.
#
# WHY A CHANNEL AND NOT A VERSION. A version pin goes stale on its own: the client's package files
# are content-addressed and replaced on every client release, so a pinned one stops existing at the
# next one. And the client updates itself from its channel anyway, so a pinned build would only have
# been the starting point of that update.
#
# No Steam Runtime is downloaded for the client: /usr/bin/steam runs it raw on the host
# (docs/steam-arm64.md). Earlier builds also named the steamrt3c runtime it ran in here.
#
# client_channel:  the arm64 client line the bootstrap installs from: the manifest
#                  steam_client_<client_channel>_linuxarm64, and its bins_linuxarm64_linuxarm64
#                  package (steamrtarm64/). It is also written to <Steam root>/package/beta, the
#                  file the client reads its own line from.
#
#                  THIS IS COUPLED TO THE LAUNCH FLAG, and the client enforces it, not us. The OGUI
#                  Steam plugin starts `steam` with -steamdeck (rootfs/overlay/usr/bin/steam). The
#                  client OWNS package/beta and repoints it to a line that matches the flag it was
#                  started with. -steamdeck keeps either steamdeck_ line; a line it does not accept
#                  gets repointed, and that costs a ~631 MB client download on the device (measured
#                  upstream on 2026-08-19, by starting a seed of another line with -steamdeck). So
#                  the bootstrap writes package/beta when it installs the client, and when the file
#                  is missing, and otherwise never touches it: once the client runs, the line is
#                  the client's. Change this and the flag together or not at all.
#
#                  Measured 2026-10-16: steamdeck_stable and Valve's default arm64 line
#                  (steam_client_linuxarm64, which the bootstrap read before) both serve build
#                  1791918634; steamdeck_publicbeta serves 1792192922.
#
#                  WHY steamdeck_stable and not steamdeck_publicbeta: publicbeta used to be the only
#                  line that gave an arm64 client what a Windows title needs. Stable at 1785799196
#                  lacked all three (Valve's FEX compat tool 3127680, the arm64 Proton 11 offer, and
#                  Steam Linux Runtime app 4185400); from 1788291500 it has them (measured upstream on
#                  hardware, 2026-09-02). If a Windows title stops launching after a client update,
#                  look for steamapps/appmanifest_4185400.acf first; setting this to
#                  steamdeck_publicbeta is the way back. Those runtimes install lazily (4185400 only
#                  once a Proton title is launched), so an empty steamapps/ on a fresh card means
#                  nothing.
#
#                  NEVER the -deckard line, although it serves newer builds. Its client asserts
#                  Steam Frame identity, which strips the guided tour's button handlers and leaves a
#                  fresh card on a welcome screen that cannot be dismissed. Its name is a gated beta
#                  branch key rather than a line name, so Valve can rotate it at any time. And under
#                  -steamdeck the client repoints away from it anyway: that is the 631 MB case above.
client_channel: steamdeck_stable
